<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apollonius of Perga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 240–c. 190 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apollonius of Perga was a Greek mathematician and astronomer from Perge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a> who worked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>. His treatise Conics, in eight books, gave a systematic theory of the conic sections and introduced the names ellipse, parabola and hyperbola. It remained the standard work on the subject for centuries and influenced later astronomers and mathematicians such as Kepler and Descartes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apollonius-of-perga.jpg" alt="Apollonius of Perga" width="248" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Battista Memo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apollonii_Pergei_Opera_1537_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
